### What is bubble sort?

Bubble sort is the simplest comparison-based sorting algorithm. It steps through the array comparing each pair of adjacent elements and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. After each complete pass, the largest unsorted element has "bubbled" to its correct position at the end.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Arrays;

public class BubbleSort {

    public static void bubbleSort(BigDecimal[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            boolean swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (arr[j].compareTo(arr[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    BigDecimal temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            if (!swapped) break; // already sorted
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] balances = {
            new BigDecimal("75000"),
            new BigDecimal("25000"),
            new BigDecimal("50000"),
            new BigDecimal("10000"),
            new BigDecimal("90000")
        };
        bubbleSort(balances);
        System.out.println("Sorted: " + Arrays.toString(balances));
    }
}
```

```text
Sorted: [10000, 25000, 50000, 75000, 90000]
```

### How does each pass work?

The outer loop counts passes. The inner loop walks adjacent pairs, comparing and swapping. After pass one, the biggest element is at the end. After pass two, the second biggest is in place. The inner loop shrinks by one each time because the tail is already sorted.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class BubbleSortTrace {

    public static void bubbleSortTrace(int[] arr) {
        int n = arr.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            boolean swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                    int temp = arr[j];
                    arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                    arr[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            System.out.println("Pass " + (i + 1) + ": " + java.util.Arrays.toString(arr));
            if (!swapped) { System.out.println("No swaps, done early!"); break; }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {50, 20, 40, 10, 30};
        bubbleSortTrace(amounts);
    }
}
```

```text
Pass 1: [20, 40, 10, 30, 50]
Pass 2: [20, 10, 30, 40, 50]
Pass 3: [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
Pass 4: [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
No swaps, done early!
```

### Why is the swapped flag important?

Without the `swapped` flag, bubble sort always performs the full n^2 work even if the array is already sorted. The flag checks whether any swap happened during a pass. If no swaps occurred, the array is sorted and the algorithm can exit early. This makes bubble sort O(n) on already-sorted or nearly-sorted input.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class EarlyExit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] sorted = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int passes = 0;
        int n = sorted.length;
        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            boolean swapped = false;
            for (int j = 0; j < n - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (sorted[j] > sorted[j + 1]) {
                    int tmp = sorted[j]; sorted[j] = sorted[j+1]; sorted[j+1] = tmp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
            passes++;
            if (!swapped) break;
        }
        System.out.println("Passes on sorted input: " + passes); // just 1
    }
}
```

```text
Passes on sorted input: 1
```

### Why is bubble sort stable?

Bubble sort is stable because it only swaps adjacent elements when the left one is strictly greater than the right one. Equal elements are never swapped past each other, so their original relative order is preserved.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class StabilityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Two transactions with same amount 50000
        String[] ids =     {"T1",    "T2",    "T3"};
        int[] amounts =    {50000,   50000,   30000};

        // Bubble sort by amount
        for (int i = 0; i < amounts.length - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < amounts.length - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (amounts[j] > amounts[j + 1]) {
                    int ta = amounts[j]; amounts[j] = amounts[j+1]; amounts[j+1] = ta;
                    String ts = ids[j]; ids[j] = ids[j+1]; ids[j+1] = ts;
                }
            }
        }
        // T1 still before T2 (both 50000): stable!
        for (int i = 0; i < ids.length; i++) {
            System.out.println(ids[i] + ": " + amounts[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
T3: 30000
T1: 50000
T2: 50000
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Case | Time | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Best | O(n) | Already sorted, one pass, no swaps |
| Average | O(n^2) | Random order |
| Worst | O(n^2) | Reverse sorted |
| Space | O(1) | In-place, only temp variable |

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class BubbleComplexity {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] sizes = {100, 1000, 10000};
        for (int n : sizes) {
            int[] arr = new int[n];
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = n - i; // worst case: reversed
            long start = System.nanoTime();
            bubbleSort(arr);
            long ms = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;
            System.out.println("n=" + n + " time=" + ms + "ms");
        }
    }

    static void bubbleSort(int[] a) {
        for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (a[j] > a[j+1]) { int t=a[j]; a[j]=a[j+1]; a[j+1]=t; }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
n=100 time=0ms
n=1000 time=3ms
n=10000 time=150ms
```

### How is bubble sort different from selection sort?

| Feature | Bubble Sort | Selection Sort |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Swap adjacent pairs | Find minimum, swap to front |
| Swaps per pass | Many (every adjacent pair) | Exactly one |
| Stable | Yes | No (basic version) |
| Early exit | Yes (swapped flag) | No (always scans fully) |
| Best on | Nearly sorted data | Write-minimising scenarios |

### When should you use bubble sort?

Never in production. Bubble sort's O(n^2) time makes it hopeless for large datasets. It is valuable as a teaching tool because it is the clearest illustration of comparison and swapping. In real banking code at the National Bank of India, always use `Collections.sort` or `Arrays.sort`.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class ProductionVsBubble {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 100_000;
        int[] data = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) data[i] = (int)(Math.random() * 1_000_000);

        int[] copy = data.clone();
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        Arrays.sort(copy);
        System.out.println("Arrays.sort: " + (System.nanoTime()-start)/1_000_000 + "ms");

        // Bubble sort on 100K would take minutes: do not attempt.
        System.out.println("Bubble sort on 100K: too slow to demonstrate.");
    }
}
```

```text
Arrays.sort: 8ms
Bubble sort on 100K: too slow to demonstrate.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the `swapped` flag, which makes bubble sort always do the full quadratic work. Another mistake is not shrinking the inner loop by `i`, which means already-sorted tail elements are re-examined on every pass.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class BubbleMistake {
    // WRONG: inner loop does not shrink
    static void buggy(int[] a) {
        for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1; j++) { // should be a.length - 1 - i
                if (a[j] > a[j+1]) { int t=a[j]; a[j]=a[j+1]; a[j+1]=t; }
            }
        }
    }

    // CORRECT: inner loop shrinks
    static void correct(int[] a) {
        for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1 - i; j++) {
                if (a[j] > a[j+1]) { int t=a[j]; a[j]=a[j+1]; a[j+1]=t; }
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Both produce correct output but buggy does more work.");
    }
}
```

```text
Both produce correct output but buggy does more work.
```
